From 221969f249be34aed590badbf0def2c5a977702b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Han <danielhanchen@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 07:59:48 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] rpc: queue split input uploads without a synchronize,
 together with the split's graph

ggml_backend_sched synchronized the split backend before every user input and
uploaded it with a blocking send. On an RPC device that serializes ~40 small
uploads per decode step, and lets other contexts' commands (a PEER_AWAIT that
blocks the server's command loop) land between a split's data and its graph.

RPC set_tensor_async already copies the data when it is called and an endpoint
runs its commands in order, so the scheduler now uploads inputs to such a
backend with set_tensor_async and no synchronize (capability queried through
the ggml_backend_set_tensor_async_copies proc address). RPC holds a split's
uploads and PEER_AWAITs back and queues them in one push with the split's
graph. A direct transfer's PEER_PUSH is tagged and queued at that moment too,
under the P2P order lock, so every endpoint still sees PUSHes and AWAITs in one
global order. Weights keep the blocking path with the hash cache.

GGML_RPC_ASYNC_INPUTS=0 or LLAMA_RPC_ASYNC_INPUTS=0 restores the old behaviour.
